  "content": {
    "text": "The company's stock surge had a secret ingredient beyond AI hype: a clever way to get someone else to pay for it.\nIf you blinked this spring, you missed it. While the S&P 500 ambled to a respectable 9.5% gain, Oracle (ORCL) quietly bolted ahead by 42.3%. The enterprise software giant, a company many investors had filed under “reliable but boring,” left peers like Microsoft and Salesforce in the dust, which returned just 1.7% and a dismal -6.1%, respectively.\nWhat happened? The easy answer is AI. The real answer is a bit more interesting. It’s about who is footing the bill for this AI gold rush.\nThe Price Of Power\nBuilding the massive data centers required for artificial intelligence is fantastically expensive. It’s a capital black hole that has spooked investors, even sparking lawsuits alleging Oracle tried to hide the “massive increases in CapEx” from the market. The fear is simple: spending billions on infrastructure today for revenue that might show up tomorrow is a great way to destroy value.\nBut on its March earnings call, the company revealed a highly effective structural shift in its financing model. Management laid out a new framework where customers are utilizing upfront capital allocation to secure their slice of the AI pie. As one executive explained, “A combination of bring-your-own-hardware and upfront customer payments enables us to continue expanding without any negative cash flow from Oracle Corporation.”\nAnd this model is already a reality. The company noted it has already “signed more than $29 billion of contracts since then” using this new approach. Suddenly, the biggest risk, a balance sheet buckling under the weight of endless construction, evaporated.\nA Different Kind Of Fuel\nWith the funding question answered, the market could focus on the staggering growth it enables. The growth velocity reported during that same quarter was unprecedented for a legacy enterprise provider. AI infrastructure revenue grew 243% year over year. Multi-cloud database revenue was up 531%. A rounding error? No, this is a fundamental shift in the business, confirmed by a colossal $553 billion backlog.\nThis new engine is pulling the whole train faster. Oracle’s overall revenue growth has accelerated to 14.9% over the last year, well above its three-year average of 10.2%. And its net margin just hit 25.3%, a three-year peak.\nThe market stopped seeing a legacy tech company taking a risky swing at AI and started seeing a shrewd operator who had convinced its customers to finance the whole venture. It’s a brilliant move, turning a capital firehose into a self-funding rocket.",
